From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 16 Nov 94 18:51:54 EST
Subject: [#94-11-236] HISTORY: Artificial Intelligence (was Quote of the day)

Craig Presson fcp@nuance.com says:

>... if you read Bellamy's _Looking
>Backward_, you get the impression of lots of big dumb machines and
>conveyor belts. I don't think either Bellamy or Wilde had the
>intellectual equipment to invent AI.

You may be correct; however, the time period during which Bellamy and Wilde
lived and wrote was actually ripe for the thoughtful investigation of the
concept of artificial intelligence.  At least one author did grapple with
the concept. Consider the chronological information below.

Edward Bellamy: US author.
Wrote ``Looking Backward: 2000-1887'' (1888)
Born 1850. Died 1898.

Oscar Wilde: Irish poet, dramatist, wit.
Wrote ``Picture of Dorian Gray'' (1891), ``Importance of Being Earnest'' (1895)
       "The Soul of Man Under Socialism" (1895), etc.
Born 1854. Died 1900.

Ambrose Bierce was a contemporaneous writer who did, in a preliminary manner,
explore the notion of artificial intelligence in the short story ``Moxon's
Master''.

Ambrose Bierce: US journalist and short story writer.
Wrote ``An Occurrence at Owl Creek Bridge'', ``Moxon's Master'' (1894)
Born 1842. Died approximately 1914.

The short story ``Moxon's Master'' is reprinted in the collection ``Machines
That Think'' edited by Isaac Asimov, Patricia Warrick and Martin Greenberg.
Below is a quote by Patricia S. Warrick from the introduction
to the story:

Stories about robots were actually quite common in nineteenth-century
literature, although the term robot was not coined until 1921, when the
Czech writer Karel Capek used the word in his play R.U.R. (Rossum's
Universal Robots). He took it from Czech robota, meaning work or compulsory
service.

Mechanical devices shaped like animals or humans had begun to pour out of
the workshops of inventors in the eighteenth century.  They were called
automata, and they came in all shapes and sizes, some as large as life.  A
duck waddled, a crouching tiger sprang upon a mechanical soldier, girls
danced, boys played musical instruments. In 1809, the German inventor
Wolfgang von Kempelen built a supposed automaton chess player that defeated
Napoleon II in a chess competition.  After Von Kempelen's death, a showman
named Maelzel toured Europe and America with the chess automaton. Edgar
Allan Poe, in a neat piece of detective work published as ``Maelzel's
Chess-Player,'' deduced that the automaton must be a fake.  Indeed, he was
right, it was eventually exposed as a fraud, with a midget inside who moved
chess pieces.

Other nineteenth-century writers before Bierce had used automata and
intelligent machines in fiction - for example, Herman Melville in ``The Bell
Tower,'' and Samuel Butler in ``The Book of Machines.'' But none seems to
have recognized the significance for man of developing this radical new
technology. Bierce's incisive mind penetrates to the heart of the issues
raised when we think about the relationship between mechanistic and living
systems. Is there a clear boundary between the animate and the inanimate,
between men and machines? Is it possible they are not dichotomous, but exist
in a continuum? Equally interesting, what is intelligence?  Should the
ability of a crystal to organize itself into a pattern be called
intelligence?  And the question of consciousness - what role does it play in
intelligence?  Could a machine ever be conscious?

``Moxon's Master'' is the first short story to raise these critical
questions.  Bierce wisely refrains from trying to answer them. No easy
answers exist. Computer scientists, physiologists, psychologists, and
philosophers are still hotly debating the same questions today.

End of quote from Patricia S. Warrick

The story, in my opinion, is not commensurate with Warrick's hyperbole but
it does treat the possibility of machine intelligence as a serious
proposition. The machine in the story can play chess and the reader
discovers it can also strangle people when irritated.  Predictably, the
sinister aspects of machine intelligence are emphasized. In Bierce's defense
we can say that his plot was not a cliche when he wrote it. Perhaps if Wilde
had lived he would have examined the topic more explicitly then he did in
the essay "The Soul of Man Under Socialism".

      Gregory Sullivan

